state: blending on an integer target is a pipeline to fix, not one to refuse.

vkd3d validates what D3D12 documents -- blending on an integer-format render
target is invalid -- and, when the shader writes that target, fails pipeline
creation with E_INVALIDARG. No shipping D3D12 driver enforces it that way. There
is nothing sensible to blend on an integer attachment, so drivers ignore the
blend state; the case most often arrives from a logic-op pipeline where those
fields are dead. Other implementations under the same pressure reached the same
answer: Ryujinx forces blend off on integer attachments, and MoltenVK has to
because Metal asserts otherwise.

So the check is correct about the specification and wrong about the world, and
the application pays: it asked for a pipeline that works on every real driver and
got back a failure it has no handling for.

MEASURED, Resident Evil Requiem, 2026-08-15. Thirteen CreateGraphicsPipelineState
calls rejected in a single session:

    vkd3d-proton:d3d12_pipeline_state_validate_blend_state: Enabling blending on
    RT 5 with format DXGI_FORMAT_R32G32B32A32_UINT, but using integer format is
    not supported.

The game stops progressing at the point it compiles shaders and ends in its own
crash reporter.

This disables blending for that attachment instead, which is what the hardware
would have done. Both cases stay WARN and stay distinguishable, because a
pipeline whose blend state was altered underneath it is worth knowing about.

diff --git a/libs/vkd3d/state.c b/libs/vkd3d/state.c
--- a/libs/vkd3d/state.c
+++ b/libs/vkd3d/state.c
@@ -4402,20 +4402,52 @@
          * in this case. If there is an output, fail pipeline compilation. */
         if (format && (format->type == VKD3D_FORMAT_TYPE_SINT || format->type == VKD3D_FORMAT_TYPE_UINT))
         {
+            /* Vessel: disable the blending rather than refuse the pipeline.
+             *
+             * Upstream splits on whether the shader writes the target: if it
+             * does not, blending is nop-ed out and the pipeline passes; if it
+             * does, this returned E_INVALIDARG. The D3D12 runtime does document
+             * blending on an integer target as invalid, so the check is not
+             * wrong -- but no shipping driver enforces it that way, blending is
+             * ignored on an integer attachment because there is nothing sensible
+             * to blend, and the case usually arrives from a logic-op state where
+             * the blend fields are dead anyway. Other implementations arrived at
+             * the same answer from the same pressure: Ryujinx forces blend off on
+             * integer attachments, and MoltenVK must because Metal asserts.
+             *
+             * The cost of being right about the spec is paid by the application,
+             * which asked for a pipeline that works everywhere else and got a
+             * failure it has no path for. A game that does not check the HRESULT
+             * then uses a NULL ID3D12PipelineState.
+             *
+             * MEASURED, Resident Evil Requiem, 2026-08-15: thirteen
+             * CreateGraphicsPipelineState calls rejected in one session --
+             *
+             *   d3d12_pipeline_state_validate_blend_state: Enabling blending on
+             *   RT 5 with format DXGI_FORMAT_R32G32B32A32_UINT, but using
+             *   integer format is not supported.
+             *
+             * -- after which the game stops progressing at the point it compiles
+             * shaders, and ends in its own crash reporter.
+             *
+             * Kept as a WARN and not silenced: a pipeline whose blend state was
+             * changed underneath it is worth knowing about, and the two cases are
+             * still distinguished so the log says which one happened.
+             */
             for (j = 0; j < sig->element_count; j++)
-            {
                 if (sig->elements[j].register_index == i)
-                {
-                    ERR("Enabling blending on RT %u with format %s, but using integer format is not supported.\n", i,
-                            debug_dxgi_format(desc->rtv_formats.RTFormats[i]));
-                    return E_INVALIDARG;
-                }
-            }
+                    break;
+
+            if (j < sig->element_count)
+                WARN("Enabling blending on RT %u with format %s, but using integer format is not supported. "
+                     "The output is written, but no driver blends an integer target, so disabling blending "
+                     "rather than failing the pipeline.\n",
+                        i, debug_dxgi_format(desc->rtv_formats.RTFormats[i]));
+            else
+                WARN("Enabling blending on RT %u with format %s, but using integer format is not supported. "
+                     "The output is not written, so nop-ing out blending.\n",
+                        i, debug_dxgi_format(desc->rtv_formats.RTFormats[i]));
 
-            /* The output does not exist, but we have to pass the pipeline. Just nop out any invalid blend state. */
-            WARN("Enabling blending on RT %u with format %s, but using integer format is not supported. "
-                 "The output is not written, so nop-ing out blending.\n",
-                    i, debug_dxgi_format(desc->rtv_formats.RTFormats[i]));
             state->graphics.blend_attachments[i].blendEnable = VK_FALSE;
         }
     }
